WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.780
Hey there! Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 115 for July 27th, 2026.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.920
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.200
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise, no jargon, let's go.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:22.160
You know that feeling when you describe exactly what you want and it actually shows up,

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.620
only this time it is a brand new game you can play in minutes.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.520
That is what is happening right now with tools that turn plain words into working game code.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:37.340
People are typing ideas like a game where you collect floating musical notes that sing when touched,

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.360
and the result runs in a browser without anyone writing lines of code by hand.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.620
Think of it like describing a new board game to a friend who instantly sketches the board,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:53.860
writes every rule, and hands you the pieces ready to use. Another way to picture it is like ordering

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:59.540
a custom pizza where the kitchen invents toppings you have never tried instead of sticking to the menu

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:05.100
everyone already knows. The systems behind this, such as Codex and Claude code,

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.660
take your description and build the full set of instructions that make the game run.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.300
This matters because it changes game creation from something that used to need years of classes

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.560
into something you can test in one afternoon. Students who love creative hobbies or wonder

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.200
about future careers in game design now get to try wild ideas without starting from zero.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.520
The same shift is turning AI into a true co-creator for art and storytelling projects too.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.360
You can try this yourself by going to the Claude site, signing up for the free option if needed,

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.160
and typing a prompt such as create a simple browser game where you collect floating musical notes

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:50.220
while avoiding spinning shapes. Add a weird twist like the notes sing when collected and watch

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:56.220
how the game changes with each new idea. The same approach works with Codex, letting you keep

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:02.500
refining the game in one sitting. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:08.980
look under the hood. The core idea starts with something you already know from texting. Your phone

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:14.840
guesses the next word based on patterns from millions of other messages. Now stretch that same

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:20.120
guess forward, but instead of one word it keeps going for whole paragraphs that build scenes,

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:26.040
characters, and game rules. Each guess gets checked against everything the system has seen before,

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:31.240
so it chooses the piece that feels most likely to fit the story you are building together.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:37.720
Think of it like a giant mixing board where tiny sound clips from thousands of old songs get rearranged

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:43.920
into a track no one has heard before. Another angle is a relay race where the baton is the last

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:50.100
sentence the system wrote, and the next runner has to continue from exactly that point. Over many

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:56.240
steps, those small choices add up into something that feels designed on purpose. The system is not

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.640
copying one existing game, it is blending patterns from thousands of games, stories, and code

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:08.020
examples it learned from earlier. That blending is why the results can feel genuinely new and sometimes

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:14.060
a little strange. The surprising part is how simple the basic move stays even when the final game

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:20.020
gets complex. When the guess is line up well, you end up with something you can actually run and test

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:26.600
in minutes. The process works because the model predicts one small chunk at a time, then uses its

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:32.520
own previous output as the new starting point. This chain of predictions lets the whole thing appear

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:38.120
without anyone writing the complete game by hand. Once you see the steps in order, the whole thing

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:44.380
feels less like magic and more like a very fast, very patient builder. Now let's move to some

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:50.980
cool stuff you can try today. New tools let you upload a book or pick one from a library and ask

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:57.200
it questions as if the book itself is answering back. Instead of flipping pages or hunting for quotes,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:02.440
you type something like, why did the main character make that choice and get a reply pulled

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:08.200
straight from the text? Think of it like having the book sitting next to you as a patient friend who

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:14.320
remembers every detail and never gets tired of explaining. Another way to see it is turning a quiet

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.360
reading session into a lively conversation that makes tough school books feel more alive.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:25.340
Anyone who reads for fun or has homework that involves analyzing stories should give it a try.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:31.640
Head to the perplexity site, paste a short public domain story or select one from the library,

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:38.160
and ask three questions about the ending or a character's motivation. Notice how the answers stay

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:44.140
grounded in the actual words instead of making things up. The tool works by matching your question

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:51.000
to relevant sections inside the book and pulling exact passages to build its reply. You can even ask

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.220
follow-up questions like, what does this scene tell us about the setting to dig deeper without losing

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:02.600
the thread? An upstate New York district decided to wait on bringing a humanoid robot into high school

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:07.600
classes after parents and students raised concerns about how it would actually be used.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:14.080
The pause came after fierce backlash. The decision gives the community time to discuss what role,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.400
if any, a robot should play in regular lessons. The story shows that even when technology feels

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:26.360
exciting, real people still get to ask whether it belongs in their daily environment. The district

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:32.740
chose to delay the rollout following the community response. Local voices shape the timeline for any

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:38.840
future classroom use. Source material appears in a Google News report on the district action.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:45.440
Researchers found that AI chatbots continue to give inconsistent or unhelpful replies when people

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:51.980
talk about anxiety, sadness or crisis moments. The study highlights ongoing challenges in how

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:58.860
these systems respond to sensitive topics, even as overall capabilities grow. The finding reminds us

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:04.760
that these tools are improving fast but are not ready to replace real support from friends, family

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:11.200
or professionals. The results point to specific gaps in handling tough mental health questions.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.460
Researchers tracked reply patterns across multiple chatbot interactions. The work underscores the

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:24.460
need for continued testing on emotional support scenarios. That's it for today. Remember every AI

